	abstract = {The 21 cm hydrogen line offers a practical way to introduce students to radio astronomy, spectral analysis, and Doppler shifts using authentic data from the Milky Way. However, the technical and financial barriers of conventional hydrogen-line detection systems often limit accessibility, particularly in resource-constrained settings. This study presents the design, implementation, and field testing of a low-cost, autonomous radio telescope system based on a Raspberry Pi and software-defined radio for detecting Doppler-shifted hydrogen emission near 1420.405 MHz. The system operates without continuous user input and records raw signal and intensity data for later spectral analysis, enabling extended observations and a post-processing workflow suitable for student use. Field trials conducted in Jasaan, Misamis Oriental, Philippines demonstrate detection of a hydrogen-line peak with both redshifted and blueshifted cases over different observing times. The results support the feasibility of an affordable and replicable set-up for physics education, public outreach, and introductory research activities. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the https://publishingsupport.iopscience.iop.org/iop-standard/v1.},
	author_keywords = {Doppler shift; hydrogen line; radio astronomy; Raspberry pi},
