	abstract = {A simple method for measuring human reaction time is presented by comparing a manually operated smartphone stopwatch with a camera-based stopwatch implemented in the Phyphox application. In the experiment, a toy car rolls down a short inclined track and the travel time is recorded simultaneously using both timing methods. While the manual stopwatch is stopped visually by the observer, the Phyphox camera stopwatch automatically detects the finish event via brightness changes in a selected sensing region. The difference between the two measured times provides an estimate of the observer’s reaction delay. To evaluate the coordination delay associated with manually starting the timing devices, the experiment was also recorded and analysed frame-by-frame using QuickTime Player. The measured start delay was small and used to correct the reaction-time values. The corrected mean reaction time obtained from repeated trials is approximately 0.13 s, consistent with reported visual reaction times. The experiment requires minimal equipment and offers a simple classroom activity to investigate human reaction time and the role of measurement methods in experimental physics. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
