	affiliations = {Department of Science Education, Universitas Sebelas Maret, Surakarta, Indonesia; Department of Educational Technology, Universitas Sebelas Maret, Surakarta, Indonesia},
	abstract = {This study aimed to develop and validate a constructed-response assessment to measure analytical thinking skills in the context of particle dynamics. Grounded in Sternberg’s theory of analytical intelligence, the instrument comprised seven open-ended items aligned with indicators such as analysing, critiquing, evaluating, explaining, logical reasoning, drawing conclusions, and sorting. A five-point rubric was developed for each item to evaluate the accuracy, depth, and logical coherence of student responses. Content validity was established through expert judgment involving six specialists in learning evaluation and physics, using Aiken’s V index. The instrument was pilot-tested with 106 undergraduate students from the Physics Education Program, and data were analysed using the Rasch model. All items demonstrated good fit (infit/outfit mean square 0.5–1.5) and high item reliability (0.99), while person reliability was moderately acceptable (0.79). The wright map confirmed good targeting, though a ceiling effect suggested limited challenge for high-performing students. Unidimensionality assumptions were satisfied, supporting construct validity. The findings provide empirical support for a theoretically grounded instrument that captures analytical thinking in physics and suggest directions for extending the assessment to broader science domains and instructional contexts. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the IOP-Standard License.},
