	abstract = {The influence of internal forces in classical mechanics is often overlooked in textbooks, despite their crucial role in shaping a system’s response to external forces. In this study, we examine a simple two-body system—a mass on a table connected by a string to a hanging mass—to highlight how internal tension affects acceleration. By varying the angle of the string, we explore how the transmission of force changes in both frictionless and frictional scenarios. Our analysis reveals that in the absence of friction, internal tension merely distributes the driving force, whereas in the presence of friction, it actively alters the normal force, thereby modifying resistive forces. These findings provide new insights into multi-body dynamics and suggest that a deeper emphasis on internal forces in introductory physics courses could enhance the presentation of Newtonian mechanics. © 2025 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
