	abstract = {Practical activities are essential for effective physics instruction; however, their implementation in secondary schools is often constrained by inadequate or unavailable laboratory apparatus. Advances in educational technology, such as virtual and blended laboratory strategies, provide alternative means for teaching physics practical. This study examined the effects of virtual laboratory and blended laboratory strategies on secondary school students’ achievement in physics practical. A quasi-experimental pretest–posttest design was employed, involving 194 Senior Secondary School II students (72 males and 122 females) selected from a population of 21 576 using a multistage sampling technique. Data were collected using the physics practical achievement test, which was validated by experts and yielded a reliability coefficient of 0.815. The instrument was administered before and after a four-week instructional intervention. Data were analysed using mean, normalised mean gain, and Analysis of Covariance. The findings revealed that students taught using the blended laboratory strategy achieved the highest normalised mean gain (25.8%), followed by those exposed to the physical laboratory method (23.6%), while the virtual laboratory group recorded the lowest gain (13.2%). The results further indicated that gender had no significant effect on students’ achievement in blended laboratory strategy (p = 0.119). The study recommends increased awareness, teacher training, and professional development workshops to promote effective implementation of blended laboratory strategy in physics practical instruction. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the https://publishingsupport.iopscience.iop.org/iop-standard/v1.},
