	abstract = {This paper presents a theoretical and epistemological reflection on the identity of physics education and on the professional practice of the physics teacher, with particular emphasis on secondary schooling. It argues that meaningful physics learning should be grounded in the heuristic structure of scientific knowledge production, rather than in the mere transmission of consolidated results or in detached discourse about science. The paper introduces the notion of Initiation to Science (ITS) as a central organising concept for physics teaching, understood as students’ engagement in inquiry practices that reflect the epistemic, historical and socio-institutional nature of scientific knowledge. From this perspective, what makes physics teaching educationally ‘modern’ is not the chronological novelty of curricular content, but the epistemological quality of the learning situations created in the classroom. The paper discusses implications for inquiry-, problem- and project-oriented approaches and argues for lean and conceptually coherent curricula organised around meaningful questions rather than exhaustive topic lists. By prioritising epistemological integrity over content accumulation, this work highlights physics education as a formative space for the development of scientific thinking. Such an approach supports students in understanding how knowledge is constructed, evaluated and legitimised, thereby fostering critical thinking, epistemic agency and a more reflective engagement with physics as a form of disciplined inquiry. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
