	abstract = {Seismology is often perceived by students as an abstract ‘big science’ confined to global networks and complex digital datasets. To bridge the cognitive gap between theoretical wave physics and real-world observation, we developed an experiential learning activity titled ‘earthquake in the courtyard’. This study utilises a professional seismic recording system for geophysical surveys to replicate a small-scale seismograph station in a university setting. Using an impulsive source (hammer-and-plate), students manually identified longitudinal (P-waves), transverse (S-waves), and dispersive surface waves. By applying site-specific velocity structures, students performed 2D triangulation methods to locate the epicentre. Results from five independent student trials demonstrate that calculating coordinates with the statistically averaged velocity structure significantly improves accuracy, yielding a low mean Euclidean distance error of 0.6 m. Furthermore, the analysis of error triangles provided a tangible framework for students to quantify experimental uncertainty and picking precision. This laboratory exercise demonstrates that high-fidelity geophysical methods can be effectively adapted into an accessible, hands-on curriculum that reinforces the scale-invariance of physical laws and the importance of site-specific empirical data. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the IOP-Standard License.},
