	affiliations = {Office Expander, Andover, MA, United States},
	abstract = {Brian Greene’s presidents-on-a-train thought experiment vividly illustrates the relativity of simultaneity in special relativity: two presidents seated at opposite ends of a moving train sign a peace treaty simultaneously in the train frame, while ground observers conclude that the signings were not simultaneous. This paper introduces a mirror arrangement at the train’s centre that reflects light from both signings outward toward the ground observers. The mirror creates an apparent paradox: the same observers who infer non-simultaneity from Greene’s original analysis now receive mirror-reflected signals together, apparently indicating simultaneity. The resolution is that the mirror cancels two asymmetries. Light from the earlier of the two presidential signings takes longer to reach the train-centre mirror, while light from the later signing takes less time. The example provides a compact classroom illustration of the distinction between what observers see and what they observe, where observing means reasoning from visual signals to physical conclusions. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the https://publishingsupport.iopscience.iop.org/iop-standard/v1.},
