	abstract = {This study investigated the potential of evidence-based physics education research (PER) strategies to improve student outcomes in South African introductory physics courses. Using students from a university in the Eastern Cape as a case study, it assessed the effectiveness of peer instruction (PI) within a traditional calculus-based introductory physics course. Think-pair-share discussion questions were integrated into lectures, and PI’s impact was evaluated through Force Concept Inventory (FCI) gains. To better understand factors influencing these gains, the study also examined the role of class attendance and scientific reasoning skills, measured using the Lawson classroom test of scientific reasoning (LCTSR). The results showed lower-than-expected normalised FCI gains for PI, with attendance and reasoning skills playing a significant moderating role. These findings suggest that for PI and other interactive engagement (IE) methods to be effective in the South African context, course design must prioritise student engagement and attendance. Moreover, the study indicates that implementing PI in isolation may not yield optimal results, and that effective integration of PI and IE strategies should be paired with efforts to develop students’ scientific reasoning abilities. Beyond evaluating the effectiveness of PI in the local context, this study also contributes to the PER literature in South Africa, by providing FCI and LCTSR scores for South African students. While the FCI and LCTSR are widely used internationally in selecting and applying PER tools in the local context, there is limited data available on South African students. © 2025 The Author(s). Published by IOP Publishing Ltd.},
