	affiliations = {College of Engineering and Technology, American University of the Middle East, Egaila, 54200, Kuwait},
	abstract = {The proliferation of advanced large language models (LLMs) in education has created new challenges for academic integrity, as students can now access AI tutors capable of solving traditional text-based problems with high accuracy. To address both conventional peer cheating and emerging AI-assisted cheating, we propose a novel method that leverages Claude AI to generate interactive animated questions integrated with Moodle’s formula question type. This approach creates multiple question versions while incorporating interactive elements that current AI systems cannot effectively process. We evaluated our method using physics problems across one-dimensional motion with friction and two-dimensional projectile motion. Our findings demonstrate that while LLMs like Claude, ChatGPT, and Gemini can readily solve traditional text-based versions of these problems, they struggle significantly with interactive versions that require user manipulation and real-time observation. The proposed method offers a scalable solution for educators to create AI-resistant assessments without requiring programming expertise, though broader validation across disciplines and AI models is needed. © 2025 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
