	affiliations = {Ministry of Education, Abu Dhabi, United Arab Emirates},
	abstract = {This study investigates alternative conceptions held by school students (Grades 6–11; approximately ages 11–16) in astronomy in Delhi, India (a context where informal explanations and cultural beliefs may shape astronomy ideas), and examines how classroom instruction influences conceptual change. A diagnostic pre-test was administered to identify prevalent misconceptions related to seasons, phases of the Moon, planets, asteroids, and the work of astronomers. Following a structured instructional intervention, an achievement post-test was conducted. Quantitative analysis of anonymized pre–post data from 150 students shows an overall improvement in performance, with a mean normalized gain of 0.47. Item-level analysis highlights concepts that respond well to instruction as well as misconceptions that remain resistant. The findings underscore the value of diagnostic assessment in astronomy education and provide evidence-based insights for improving instructional strategies in school-level science classrooms in similar contexts. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the https://publishingsupport.iopscience.iop.org/iop-standard/v1.},
