	abstract = {Climate literacy is a key educational objective in response to climate crisis, including an understanding of the scientific principles underlying climate change, such as the greenhouse effect. To address this need, we designed a learning environment on the greenhouse effect for lower secondary education within a design-based research (DBR) framework. Across five iterative design cycles, the learning environment was developed, implemented, evaluated, and systematically refined. The design was informed by learning theory, multimedia principles, and empirical research on students’ conceptions. Each iteration was implemented and evaluated with lower secondary students and refined accordingly. After participating in the implementation of the final version of the learning environment, 92 eighth grade students from Austria positively developed their understanding of the greenhouse effect, using a validated test instrument, with a large effect size. Four to six weeks later, students still demonstrated significantly higher scores, with a medium effect size, indicating lasting learning gains. These findings suggest that the learning environment effectively supports sustained conceptual understanding of the greenhouse effect in the target age group. All materials are available in English and German. © 2025 The Author(s). Published by IOP Publishing Ltd.},
