	affiliations = {School of Mathematics and Physics, University of Queensland, St. Lucia, 4072, QLD, Australia; UQ College, University of Queensland, St. Lucia, 4072, QLD, Australia},
	abstract = {There are various locations in the vicinity of the Earth’s orbit around the Sun where centripetal and centrifugal forces are balanced and spacecraft are able to orbit the Sun at the same period as the Earth. These locations are known as Lagrange points, numbered 1-5. The first of these, L1, lies about 1.5 million km from the Earth towards the Sun. Various spacecraft, for example the solar and heliospheric observatory (SOHO) and deep space climate observatory (DSCOVR) sit in halo obits around L1. SOHO provides high resolution images of the Sun, and DSCOVR provides an early warning of coronal mass ejections, and detailed images of the Earth. In a halo orbit, the orthogonal components of the spacecraft-Sun and spacecraft-Earth force vectors provide the centripetal force enabling a spacecraft to orbit a point in space. This is essentially the same physics as in a skipping rope, which can be demonstrated in the classroom using a skipping rope. © 2025 The Author(s). Published by IOP Publishing Ltd.},
