	abstract = {This three-year pre-post study investigated changes in undergraduate forensic science students’ attitudes and beliefs about physics during a four-week interactive block-format course in Physics in Forensic Sciences. A total of 216 s-year students completed the Colorado Learning Attitudes about Science Survey (CLASS) at the start and end of instruction, providing both personal responses and estimates of a perceived expert-like view. Personal favourable responses increased from 40.1% to 61.3% (gain = 21.2%, 95% CI [18.46, 23.94], Cohen’s d = 0.50, Hake’s g = 0.35). Perceived expert-like responses increased from 72.6% to 91.4% (gain = 18.8%, 95% CI [16.64, 20.96], Cohen’s d = 0.98, Hake’s g = 0.69). Positive shifts were observed in all CLASS categories, with the largest gains in Problem Solving Sophistication (+24.2%) and Real World Connections (+22.9%). Female students entered with lower personal scores than male students but showed larger gains, narrowing the initial gap. In the absence of a control group or historical course data, the findings are interpreted as evidence that the instructional design may have contributed to more favourable beliefs when physics was consistently connected to forensic practice. © 2026 The Author(s). Published by IOP Publishing Ltd. Original content from this work may be used under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 license. Any further distribution of this work must maintain attribution to the author(s) and the title of the work, journal citation and DOI.},
