	abstract = {A simple classroom experiment is described that uses a marble rolling down an inclined plane to teach students the relationship between gravitational potential energy, and linear and rotational kinetic energy. A marble is rolled down a 1 m-long aluminium channel set at five inclinations, and a mobile phone video camera used to measure the time for the marble to roll from the top to the bottom of the track. Uniform acceleration of the marble is assumed. The acceleration time is used to calculate the final linear velocity and kinetic energy knowing the mass of the marble. The linear velocity is used to calculate the angular velocity of the marble, assuming no slippage. The sum of the linear and rotation energies is plotted against initial potential energy. The experiment is useful for teaching students about conservation of energy, and the role this has played in major discoveries in physics, for example the discovery of the neutrino, dark matter, and dark energy. © 2026 The Author(s). Published by IOP Publishing Ltd. Original content from this work may be used under the terms of the https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/. Any further distribution of this work must maintain attribution to the author(s) and the title of the work, journal citation and DOI.},
