	abstract = {The second Newton law is one of the basic laws in classical mechanics and therefore, demands deep conceptual understanding by physics learners. This, however, frequently is not the situation. Precisely, students experience difficulties applying Newton Laws in the analysis of real life situations. An interesting real-world context ruled by the principles of classical mechanics is the world of acrobatics and parkour. Here almost every jump creates the ‘Wow’ affect and hence is probable to draw the attention of learners; thus, inspiring a true curiosity regarding its underlying physics principles. Each acrobatic jump requires the skill of safe landing which is the process of zeroing a jumpers’ velocity within a certain time period. The second Newton law describes this situation. In the present study, we analyse a real jump and employ it as a real life case for 5 clinical interviews and 3 focus-group interviews (conducted as whole class dialogic discussions)—69 participants in total. The interviews’ analysis yielded three kinds of misconceptions. We further discuss these misconceptions and suggest practical ways for their review. We believe that physics teaching and classical mechanics precisely, should be based on such practical examples, which are likely to arouse curiosity on the one hand, and can be easily experienced by most of the learners. In our case, the tool is always accessible; it is the body of every one of us. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
