	abstract = {This study aims to integrate and evaluate the effectiveness of a metaverse Spatial.io-based pre-laboratory environment in enhancing students’ conceptual understanding of Newton’s laws. Conventional pre-laboratory media are often passive and provide limited opportunities for immersive, interactive preparation, which may restrict students’ ability to develop a robust conceptual foundation before examination. A quasi-experimental pretest–posttest control group design was employed, involving two groups of physics education students (experimental n = 30; control n = 30). Conceptual understanding was assessed using a validated and reliable test instrument. The results indicate that the experimental group engaging with Spatial.io achieved significantly greater improvement in conceptual understanding than the control group using 2D video media, with an independent sample t-test showing a significant difference in normalizedgains (t (58) = −3.199, p = 0.002). These findings suggest that immersive pre-laboratory experience can better facilitate active cognitive engagement and support the formation of more concrete mental models of fundamental mechanics concepts. Overall, Spatial.io appears feasible and promising as an innovative pre-laboratory medium that bridges theory and practice in physics learning and offers a scalable approach to strengthening conceptual readiness before laboratory activities. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the IOP-Standard License.},
