	affiliations = {Gazi University, Department of Physics Education, Gazi Education Faculty, Ankara, Turkey},
	abstract = {Refraction of light is one of the important topics in physics education. Depending on how the refraction is defined, textbooks offer different opinions on whether light waves travel perpendicular to the interface between two media and undergo refraction when passing from one medium to another. We examined twenty-eight fundamental physics textbooks to reveal the differences of opinion in the literature regarding the definition of refraction of light in a single source. It was observed that the majority of textbooks (57.1%) defined refraction of light waves as “the change in direction at the interface.” Additionally, the refraction was randomly defined as “the change in average speed”, “the bending”, or “the transmission” of light when passing from one medium to another. Therefore, some textbooks thought that light waves were not refracted when their direction of travel was perpendicular to the surface, despite changes in the medium. However, 42.9% of textbooks defined the refraction of light waves as “the change in the average speed and wavelength when passing from one medium to another”, and they thought that light was refracted even when it was perpendicular to the surface, regardless of the angle of incidence. © 2025 The Author(s). Published by IOP Publishing Ltd.},
	author_keywords = {literature review; refracted wave; refraction; Snell’s laws},
