	abstract = {This paper presents an integrated sequence of introductory physics laboratory activities designed to promote modelling, graphical analysis, and uncertainty reasoning. The approach combines pairs of complementary experiments for the determination of liquid density, the elastic constant of a spring, and gravitational acceleration, while emphasizing the common methodological framework used to analyze experimental data. In each case, theoretical relationships are reformulated into linear forms that allow experimental results to be interpreted through graphical models, with physical parameters obtained from the slopes of fitted lines. The activities also highlight the process of transforming nonlinear relationships into linear representations and comparing results obtained through independent methods. Rather than focusing solely on the numerical determination of physical constants, the proposed sequence emphasizes modelling, data interpretation, and the role of experimental uncertainty in evaluating physical models. Because the experiments rely on standard laboratory equipment and widely accessible measurement techniques, the framework can be readily implemented in undergraduate teaching laboratories to support the development of fundamental experimental skills. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
