	abstract = {Understanding electrical circuits remains a persistent challenge in physics education, particularly in technical–vocational contexts where laboratory resources may be limited. This study investigates the impact of an inquiry-based instructional intervention using a black-box experimental methodology on students’ conceptual understanding, confidence, and motivation related to electrical circuit laws. The study was conducted in a technical secondary school in Angola, providing evidence from an educational context that remains underrepresented in physics education research. A quasi-experimental pre-test/post-test control group design was implemented with 70 Grade 11 students (typically aged 16–17 years) enrolled in an Energy and Electrical Installations programme. The experimental group engaged in hands-on black-box investigations designed to promote model-based reasoning using Ohm’s Law and Kirchhoff’s Laws, while the control group received traditional lecture-based instruction. Students’ responses were collected using a five-point Likert-scale questionnaire and analysed using Mann–Whitney U and Wilcoxon Signed-Rank tests. Pre-test results showed no statistically significant differences between groups (p > 0.05), indicating comparable initial levels. After the intervention, statistically significant differences were observed between groups across all measured constructs (Q1–Q3: p < 0.001; Q4: p = 0.012). Effect-size analysis revealed large effects for confidence, application of circuit laws, and conceptual understanding (r = 0.72–0.75) and a moderate effect for motivation (r = 0.30). Additional post-test survey items applied only to the experimental group indicated very positive student perceptions of the activity (Q5 mean = 4.89, SD = 0.32; Q6 mean = 4.97, SD = 0.17). These results suggest that black-box experimental activities provide an effective and low-cost strategy for promoting inquiry-based learning and improving conceptual understanding of electrical circuits in resource-constrained technical education environments. The findings highlight the potential of black-box investigations as an accessible experimental strategy for teaching electrical circuits in technical and secondary physics classrooms. © 2026 The Author(s). Published by IOP Publishing Ltd. Original content from this work may be used under the terms of the https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/. Any further distribution of this work must maintain attribution to the author(s) and the title of the work, journal citation and DOI.},
