	affiliations = {University of Rzeszów, Faculty of Exact and Technical Sciences, Institute of Physics, Pigonia 1 Street, Rzeszów, 35-310, Poland; Faculty of Science, University of Helsinki, Gustaf Hallströmin katu 2, Helsinki, FI-00014, Finland; Institute of Physics and Technology, Ural Federal University, Mira str. 19, Ekaterinburg, 620002, Russian Federation},
	abstract = {A team of students from the University of Rzeszów observed the partial solar eclipse of 29 March 2025 under clear skies in southeastern Poland. Using three independent optical systems-a projection setup, a digital camera equipped with a Baader solar filter, and a Coudé 150/2250 refractor with a glass filter-we documented the progression of the eclipse and analyzed the observational results. The project was designed to evaluate how structured, hands-on observing activities can support the development of practical astronomical skills and enhance student engagement with scientific inquiry. In the phase of maximum eclipse, the magnitude was k = 0.097, which means that 9.7% of the Sun’s diameter was obscured. From this, a model was used to estimate the perceived change in solar brightness, yielding a subjective decrease of 0.11 magnitudes. Although this phenomenon was a shallow eclipse, it provides a useful quantitative exercise in relating eclipse geometry to radiant flux and visual magnitude. Overall, the event allowed students to practice safe solar-observing techniques, analyze real astronomical data, and develop skills applicable to future eclipse observations and other time-dependent astronomical phenomena. © 2026 The Author(s). Published by IOP Publishing Ltd. Original content from this work may be used under the terms of the https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/. Any further distribution of this work must maintain attribution to the author(s) and the title of the work, journal citation and DOI.},
