	affiliations = {ELTE Eötvös Loránd University, Institute of Physics and Astronomy, Budapest, Hungary; Horry County Schools, Conway, SC, United States},
	abstract = {Understanding Newton’s laws is often challenging for students due to persistent misconceptions and the abstract nature of the concepts involved. This study explores the use of model rocketry within a teaching sequence structured according to Kolb’s experiential learning cycle. A teaching unit on Newtonian mechanics was implemented with three groups of secondary school students: a traditional instruction group, a Kolb cycle–based activity group (KOLB), and a Kolb cycle–based group that additionally participated in a model rocket project (ROCKET). Students’ conceptual understanding was assessed in a quasi-experimental design using a researcher-developed knowledge test administered before and after the instructional unit. The results were analysed using a repeated measures analysis of variance. A significant improvement between pre-test and post-test scores was observed across all groups. However, the interaction between time and instructional group was not statistically significant, indicating that the magnitude of learning gains did not differ significantly between the instructional approaches. Students’ perceptions of the rocket project were also examined through selected items from an end-of-year questionnaire. A large majority of students in the ROCKET group identified the rocket project as their favourite physics learning activity. The findings suggest that instruction structured according to Kolb’s experiential learning cycle can be as effective as traditional instruction in supporting students’ understanding of Newtonian mechanics. While the rocket project did not produce a measurable additional effect on learning gains, student feedback indicates that it can provide an engaging context for learning physics. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the https://publishingsupport.iopscience.iop.org/iop-standard/v1.},
