	abstract = {The rising pitch heard during water-bottle filling is a simple yet instructive example of resonance in a time-varying fluid–air system. This study presents a theoretical analysis of the relationship between water height, discharge rate, and sound frequency by modelling the bottle as a closed-end organ pipe. As the bottle fills, the water–air interface rises and continuously shortens the air column, which acts as a variable-length resonator whose fundamental frequency increases accordingly. Using an idealised, monotonically decreasing volumetric inflow (taken here as linear in time) and neglecting turbulence and Doppler effects, we derive an analytical frequency–time expression. The resulting function predicts a nonlinear frequency increase consistent with standing-wave physics in a compressible medium. For representative parameters H = 0.23 m, A = 3.3 × 10<sup>−3</sup> m<sup>2</sup>, and c = 340 m s<sup>−1</sup>, the predicted pitch evolution aligns with everyday observations of bottle filling. Although the governing equation reduces to a Chini-type form that does not generally admit a closed-form solution, a series approximation provides useful insight into its behaviour. The model clarifies how fluid filling kinematics sets a time-dependent acoustic boundary condition, offering an accessible theoretical context for teaching resonance in physics. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
