	abstract = {This study investigates how GeoGebra and PhET simulations are integrated into mathematics and physics curricula in rural and urban Rwandan lower secondary schools, focusing on both the opportunities they present and the challenges teachers encounter. The study is guided by the following research questions: (i) To what extent are GeoGebra and PhET simulations being used in the teaching and learning of mathematics and physics in rural and urban lower secondary schools in Rwanda? (ii) What are the challenges hindering the effective use of GeoGebra and PhET simulations in the teaching and learning of mathematics and physics in Rwandan lower secondary schools? A mixed-methods approach was employed, combining surveys and interviews with mathematics and physics teachers across selected schools. Quantitative results indicate that while a majority of teachers are aware of GeoGebra and PhET simulations, approximately 56% do not use them regularly, mainly due to limited information and communication technology (ICT) skills, time constraints, and insufficient computer and internet facilities. Qualitative findings further highlight teachers’ perceived benefits of simulations in enhancing conceptual understanding and learner engagement. The study concludes that systematic professional development in digital pedagogy and improved ICT infrastructure are essential for promoting the effective integration of interactive simulations in science, technology, engineering, and mathematics classrooms. These findings have implications for educational policy and teacher training initiatives aimed at advancing technology-enhanced learning in Rwanda. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
