	abstract = {Laboratory activities aimed at teaching thermodynamic concepts often rely on simplified experimental setups, particularly in educational contexts with limited resources. One common consequence of such conditions is the appearance of systematic discrepancies between theoretical and experimental values, especially when calorimetric measurements are performed using open or imperfect systems. This paper analyses a set of temperature measurements obtained from the dissolution of sodium hydroxide (NaOH) in water using improvised calorimeters. Rather than seeking to optimise calorimetric accuracy, the study characterises the systematic error inherent to open systems and examines its potential pedagogical significance in the teaching of enthalpy. A simple model incorporating a corrective term, (Formula presented) (Formula presented), is introduced to represent heat losses to the environment. The analysis suggests that non-ideal experimental conditions can provide a structured context for discussing systematic error, model assumptions and non-ideal behaviour in thermodynamics. In addition, an open-access web application is presented to facilitate exploratory analysis of how variations in temperature measurements influence calculated enthalpy values. The work constitutes a theoretically grounded instructional proposal based on experimental analysis and illustrates how a modelling framework can support reflective discussion of systematic error in resource-limited laboratory settings. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the https://publishingsupport.iopscience.iop.org/iop-standard/v1.},
