	affiliations = {Faculty of Science, University of Split, Split, Croatia; Faculty of Physics, University of Rijeka, Rijeka, Croatia},
	abstract = {This article presents a simple and accurate method for determining the gravitational acceleration g by applying Archimedes’ principle. A cylindrical aluminium rod was suspended and gradually immersed in water, and a digital scale recorded the buoyant force at each depth. The volume of the immersed rod was calculated geometrically with a correction applied to the finite vessel size. By plotting the buoyant force against volume and using the known density of water, the value of g was extracted from the slope of the best-fit line. The result, g = 9.788 ± 0.023 m s<sup>−2</sup>, agreed well with the accepted value and demonstrated the educational potential of fluid-based alternatives to traditional kinematic methods. This experiment offers a low-cost and conceptually rich opportunity to explore hydrostatics, Newtonian mechanics, and metrology in an integrated manner. As such, it represents a valuable and accessible method for high school physics laboratories. © 2025 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
