	affiliations = {Escola de Especialistas de Aeronáutica, SP, Guaratinguetá, Brazil},
	abstract = {In technical aircraft maintenance courses, concepts such as gyroscopic rigidity are often taught descriptively, without recourse to mathematical or physical formalisation. This study, conducted within a military technical school (Escola de Especialistas de Aeronáutica, Brazil), examined the mental models developed by students after exposure to such an approach, using the attitude indicator—an aircraft navigation instrument—as a contextualising element. The research followed a qualitative, interaction-based methodology grounded in Vygotsky’s sociohistorical framework. Students were first presented with a typical descriptive explanation of gyroscopic rigidity from the technical manuals. They were then asked to graphically represent the behaviour of the rotation of a free gyroscope at different latitudes. Instructor and peer interactions were encouraged, and targeted mediations were applied according to the models initially developed by the students. Two distinct student models have emerged. The first model interpreted rigidity as relative to the earth’s horizontal, meaning that the rotation axis would remain horizontal regardless of latitude. The second model aligned more closely with the physical principle of absolute rigidity in space, wherein the rotation axis maintained its initial orientation independent of latitude. The descriptive approach commonly used in technical texts can lead to divergent and incomplete mental models of gyroscopic rigidity. Effective learning requires structured mediation to address these specific misconceptions, particularly through hands-on interactions with physical instruments and peer dialogue. This study highlights the importance of adapting instruction to students’ cultural and educational backgrounds, especially in heterogeneous classrooms, to foster an accurate conceptual understanding of technical physics education. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar te