	abstract = {Quantum physics plays a central role in contemporary science and technology, while school textbooks mediate curriculum intentions and classroom practice. This study examines how quantum physics concepts are represented in Greek upper secondary physics and chemistry textbooks. A qualitative content analysis was conducted on the officially prescribed student textbooks used in all three grades of the Greek General Lyceum. The analysis investigated the presence, distribution, pedagogical framing, and cross-disciplinary coherence of quantum concepts across subjects and grade levels. The findings indicate that quantum content is largely absent in the first two grades of physics and becomes concentrated in the final grade, where it is predominantly presented descriptively. In chemistry, quantum concepts appear earlier but are mainly embedded within application-oriented discussions of atomic structure. Across both subjects, probabilistic and epistemological dimensions receive limited attention, and cross-disciplinary alignment remains weak. These patterns suggest that quantum theory is presented through parallel disciplinary trajectories rather than as an integrated conceptual framework. © 2026 The Author(s). Published by IOP Publishing Ltd. Original content from this work may be used under the terms of the https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/. Any further distribution of this work must maintain attribution to the author(s) and the title of the work, journal citation and DOI.},
