	abstract = {Negative attitudes of secondary school students toward Physics, particularly in the topic of linear momentum, remain a critical barrier to effective learning. This study investigated the effect of enhanced spaced learning (ESL) on students’ attitudes toward Physics, with gender as a moderating factor. A pretest–posttest control group quasi-experimental design was adopted. The sample comprised 87 Senior Secondary School II students purposively selected from two public schools in Ibadan North Local Government Area, Oyo State, Nigeria, and randomly assigned to either the ESL group (n = 57) or the conventional teaching group (n = 30). The Physics students’ attitude questionnaire scale (reliability coefficient = 0.83) and instructional guides for both strategies were employed. Data were analysed using analysis of covariance at a 0.05 level of significance and estimated marginal mean. Findings revealed a significant main effect of treatment on students’ attitudes toward Physics, F (1, 83) = 8.309, p = .005, partial η <sup>2</sup> = 0.089, favouring the ESL group (M = 0.821) over the control group (M = 0.763). However, gender had no significant main effect, F(1, 83) = 0.969, p = .328, partial η <sup>2</sup> = 0.012. The study concludes that ESL is effective in fostering more positive attitudes toward Physics regardless of gender and recommends its integration into classroom practice for challenging Physics topics. © 2025 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
