	abstract = {Analogies are used extensively in physics education. In this paper an elastic band analogy is used to teach students about the physical principles behind the generation of coronal mass ejections (CMEs), which are massive balls of plasma ejected from the Sun. If the Earth lies in the path of a CME the plasma interacts with the magnetosphere of the Earth. CMEs are of increasing concern due to their potential to disrupt global telecommunications. In this paper, the basic equations of motion are applied to a CME. Some CMEs are able to accelerate from zero metres per second to 1% of the speed of light in the space of the radius of the Sun in a period of about 7 min. A twisted elastic band on a toy plane is an example of how twisted material can store potential energy which can then be converted into kinetic energy. A stretched and flicked elastic band is another analogy to CMEs. Elastic bands are also a good model of the closed nature of magnetic field lines. © 2025 The Author(s). Published by IOP Publishing Ltd.},
