	abstract = {This manuscript presents a hands-on experiment designed to help students understand the relationship between mechanical deformation and thermal changes in materials. Using a simple balloon, students observe the effect of stretching and relaxing when it leads to variations in surface temperature, which can be explained through fundamental concepts such as energy transfer and entropy changes. The balloon is incrementally stretched up to 45 cm, during which its surface temperature increases from room temperature (25 °C) to a maximum of 38 °C. Upon relaxing, the temperature drops to as low as 15 °C. These results demonstrate that mechanical strain can induce significant thermal effects, highlighting the practical application of thermodynamics, this effect is also known as Elastocaloric effect. By analyzing the experimental data, students can develop a deeper understanding that mechanical forces can influence thermal behavior. The experiment provides an accessible introduction to energy transformations and the physics of materials, fostering engagement through direct observation and real-world connections. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
