	abstract = {Thermoelectric generators provide an accessible context for teaching energy conversion because their electrical output varies dynamically with thermal conditions and circuit loading. This paper presents a thermoelectric real-time measurement system (TRMS) as a classroom-ready approach that makes thermoelectric current visible in real time using an Arduino-compatible Wi-Fi microcontroller and Blynk mobile visualisation. In two related configurations, the TRMS supports temperature-gradient monitoring and extended electrical characterisation, allowing temperature, voltage, current, power, and energy to be displayed on a smartphone dashboard as live numerical indicators and time-series plots. Pedagogically, the approach helps students relate temperature difference, load, and electrical response during heating and cooling, rather than treating measurements as isolated values. The TRMS can be used as a ready-to-run instrument for guided inquiry or as a student-built system for measurement-design and project-based learning. Two model investigations are presented to illustrate classroom implementation. The approach offers a low-cost and adaptable alternative for real-time thermoelectric experimentation, while also highlighting practical considerations of Wi-Fi-based mobile logging in school settings. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the https://publishingsupport.iopscience.iop.org/iop-standard/v1.},
