	abstract = {Students often rely on intuitive reasoning when interpreting motion in introductory mechanics. However, many physical situations produce results that contradict these intuitive expectations. Such discrepancies can create valuable learning opportunities when explored through visual and computational tools. This paper presents a set of Python-based simulations designed to investigate several counterintuitive phenomena in high school mechanics. The simulations illustrate cases where common assumptions about motion fail, including projectile motion launched from an elevated position, comparisons of straight and curved descent paths, and situations in which a slower object overtakes a faster one due to different acceleration conditions. Using accessible computational tools implemented in Python, the simulations generate clear visualisations including trajectory plots, position–time graphs, velocity–time graphs and phase-space diagrams. These visual representations allow students to explore relationships between position, velocity and acceleration while comparing intuitive predictions with computational results. The approach demonstrates how accessible computational simulations can support conceptual understanding in introductory physics and encourage students to critically examine their intuition about motion. The simulations are designed to be easily implemented in classroom activities, allowing students to explore counterintuitive mechanics through computational modelling. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the https://publishingsupport.iopscience.iop.org/iop-standard/v1.},
