	abstract = {Teachers’ professional growth is grounded in a cumulative, reflective process embedded in their day-to-day practice, which often reflects a compromise between competing instructional goals and the constraints teachers face. Carrying out instructional practices that advance their educational goals often requires teachers to implement changes and step out of their comfort zone, a cognitively and emotionally demanding process. Professional development programs that are ongoing, challenging, focused on the subject matter and student learning, and are situated in teachers’ practice can support these change processes. The goal of the current study was to characterize a sustainable, effective large-scale professional development program—a national network of professional learning communities (PLCs) for high school physics teachers. The program has been operating continuously since 2012, and encompasses a population of about a quarter of the total national workforce (N ≈ 275). The program is led by a team of researchers and experienced high school physics teachers from a university-based physics education research group, as a research-practice partnership. This study had two aims. We first describe the key features of the program and articulate its operational model and then characterize the evolving design of disciplinary learning themes within the PLCs. This long-term, longitudinal study employed a design-based research methodology. The results show that the sustainability and effectiveness of the PLCs program as a long-term, large-scale professional development framework for physics teachers is closely tied to its ongoing renewal, as well as to the strength of the research-practice partnership. Overall, the findings illustrate how a learning community can effectively foster the professional growth of teachers and teacher-leaders and suggest that PLC networks can serve as leverage to promote reform initiatives in science education. © 2025 The Author(s). Publi