	abstract = {This paper explores the application of the Action–Process–Object–Schema (APOS) framework to analyse and enhance conceptual understanding in science education, specifically in thermodynamics, kinematics, and magnetism. Results show that while most students excel at the Action level, performance significantly declines at higher cognitive stages: Process (40%–54%), Object (43%–54%), and Schema (23%–31%). The APOS framework offers valuable insights into students’ cognitive development, with its strengths lying in clear diagnostic modelling and instructional guidance through the ACE cycle (Activities, Classroom discussion, Exercises). However, its focus on individual cognition limits its consideration of affective, social, and contextual factors in learning. This paper argues that integrating APOS with complementary pedagogical approaches can promote deeper, more integrated learning experiences, facilitating students’ progression to higher levels of conceptual understanding. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
