	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85219380480?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Liceo Scientifico ‘E. Fermi’, Ragusa, Italy},
	abstract = {In physics education, understanding error theory is essential for both epistemological and practical reasons, particularly when students encounter experimental uncertainties in laboratory settings. For direct measurements, when random uncertainties were negligible, the experimental error was estimated using instrumental resolution. However, when significant random uncertainties are present, they are often estimated using a half-range formula. For indirect measurements, the traditional approach involves the error propagation theory, which can be challenging for first-year high school students because of its complexity. An alternative approach involves estimating the experimental uncertainty a posteriori using a half-range formula. This method simplifies error estimation and is more accessible to students, potentially enhancing their comprehension and the application of error analysis in experimental physics. © 2024 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
