	affiliations = {Department of Physics Education, Julius-Maximilians-University, Würzburg, Germany},
	abstract = {The use of digital support materials is becoming increasingly popular in teaching. One promising use for teaching physics is the joint presentation of a real-life experiment and the theoretical model utilised to explain the experiment’s phenomenon. This holistic approach facilitates the creation of a mental link between model and reality, supporting the students’ understanding and learning. The ‘PUMA : Spannungslabor’ app was developed to enable this approach, allowing it to be used when teaching about simple electric circuits. The application visualises the base quantities voltage (abstracted via a height- or pressure-analogy based model), current (abstracted via moving electrons) and electrical resistance (abstracted via a collision-analogy based model). The visualisations can be shown either via a video-see-through augmented reality (AR) mode (in combination with a real experiment setup) or as a fully digital simulation. To evaluate the impact the app’s use in teaching has on the learning process, a student laboratory study was conducted with N = 441 8th grade students (ages 13–14). The empirical results revealed a significant difference in the development of conceptual knowledge between using the app as AR or as a simulation. Additionally, it was found that using the AR-app to display model representations lowered the students’ cognitive load and its use for data acquisition significantly reduced the amount of time the students needed to finish their tasks. These results provide information for a target group-oriented design and implementation of AR-apps for school lessons. The benefits of using this AR-app are currently examined in an intervention study in classroom practice. A teaching unit was developed which utilises the app at various points to support the students’ learning process. This course, together with the AR-app, simulation and additional supplementary material, is available free of charge for students and teachers on the website https