	abstract = {The geomagnetic storm of 10–11 May 2024, popularly known as the Mother’s Day storm, was one of the most intense space-weather events of the current solar cycle, reaching G5 (Extreme) levels on the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration scale with a Dst (disturbance storm-time) index minimum of −412 nT. This study presents a comprehensive analysis of the storm’s global and regional responses in vertical total electron content, highlighting hemispheric asymmetries and variations in recovery time. Using freely available data sources and public-domain platforms, we demonstrate how this event provides an authentic, real-world context for exploring electromagnetic concepts such as plasma, field–particle interactions, and space-weather effects on communication systems. The pedagogical framework emphasises inquiry-based and data-driven learning approaches that allow students to connect textbook physics with natural phenomena. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
