	affiliations = {Faculdade de Engenharia da Computação e Telecomunicações, Instituto de Tecnologia, Universidade Federal do Pará, Pará, Belém, 66075-110, Brazil},
	abstract = {The teaching of geometric optics involving spherical surfaces is generally limited, for pedagogical reasons, to the paraxial approximation in elementary physics textbooks, as well as in virtual simulation platforms. However, quite often, these didactic materials end up excessively stretching the validity of the approximation, applying it in situations in which it could not be simply applied, possibly generating misconceptions about the physical phenomena being conveyed. In this work, we present the development of two interactive simulations from scratch, using GeoGebra, aimed at teaching geometric optics exploring didactically the paraxial approximation limits. The first simulation explores the process of image formation in spherical mirrors, similarly to what is done in textbooks, but not avoiding the distortions in the images (optical aberrations) that occur beyond the validity regime of the paraxial approximation. The second simulation aims to didactically explore how far we can stretch the validity of the approximation while maintaining its reasonableness, and what happens beyond that. It provides an in-depth examination of the conditions for the formation of a focus, specifically considering a hemispherical shaped mirror. We follow a flexible approach in the construction process of the simulations, so that the teacher may implement their own adjustments, according to their specific teaching objectives, without the need for programming skills. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the https://publishingsupport.iopscience.iop.org/iop-standard/v1.},
