	abstract = {A low-cost experimental method for investigating blackbody radiation is presented using a heated filament and smartphone-based colour detection. The red, green, and blue intensities of emitted light were measured as a function of electrical power. Analysis of the intensity ratios shows that the blue-to-red (B/R) and blue-to-green (B/G) ratios increase with increasing power, whereas the red-to-green (R/G) ratio decreases. These trends indicate a progressive shift of the emitted spectrum towards shorter wavelengths as the filament temperature increases. The observations are in qualitative agreement with Planck’s law and Wien’s displacement law. A comparison with theoretical trends suggests that the iPhone 12 Pro Max camera used in this study can function as a three-channel, low-resolution spectrometer. To assess measurement consistency, the experiment was repeated using two independent iPhone applications (physics toolbox and colorimeter), which produced similar qualitative trends. The method provides an accessible approach for exploring thermal radiation in classroom settings. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the IOP-Standard License.},
