	affiliations = {Institute of Physics, University of Brasília, Brasília, Brazil},
	abstract = {This paper examines the role of entropy in supporting an Initiation to Science within secondary physics education. It is argued that conventional approaches, often centred on problem-solving and formal manipulation, tend to weaken the epistemological meaning of key concepts, limiting students’ engagement with the nature of scientific knowledge. In contrast, a conceptual approach to entropy is proposed, in which its introduction is grounded in questions of irreversibility, probabilistic reasoning and the relationship between microscopic and macroscopic descriptions. Rather than presenting entropy as a formal quantity to be applied algorithmically, the approach emphasises its role within a model-based explanatory framework. Illustrative classroom situations are discussed to show how students can be engaged in interpreting physical processes through statistical reasoning, without relying on prescriptive instructional sequences. It is argued that, when approached in this way, entropy provides a productive context for initiating students into central features of scientific thinking, contributing to a broader understanding of physics education as an entry into the epistemological practices of science. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the https://publishingsupport.iopscience.iop.org/iop-standard/v1.},
