	abstract = {This study presents a theatre-based embodied learning approach for addressing students’ misconceptions in electric circuits within a non-formal educational setting. Eighteen secondary school students (aged 15–16) participated in a science club, where they explored electric circuits through the collaborative development and performance of a theatrical play. The approach aimed to engage students in reflecting on and externalising their alternative conceptions through embodied representations. Students initially explored their ideas through discussion, experiments, and simulations, focusing on key concepts such as the role of the source, electron movement, and the function of the switch. They subsequently developed and performed a theatrical play representing both misconceptions and scientifically accepted models at macroscopic and microscopic levels. Data were collected through semi-structured group interviews and analysed using thematic analysis. Findings suggest that students perceived the embodied and theatrical representations as helping them reflect on and reconsider their initial ideas about electric circuits, while also fostering metacognitive awareness. In addition, students described the process as engaging and supportive of collaboration, creativity, communication, and critical reflection. The study suggests that theatre-based embodied learning can serve as a promising complementary approach for teaching abstract physics concepts, particularly those involving non-observable phenomena. © 2026 The Author(s). Published by IOP Publishing Ltd. Original content from this work may be used under the terms of the https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/. Any further distribution of this work must maintain attribution to the author(s) and the title of the work, journal citation and DOI.},
