	abstract = {Students’ recurring difficulties in learning reflect to some degree the weakness in an instructor’s pedagogy. This study assesses students’ performance in introductory calculus-based physics, focusing on mechanics, among freshmen at a top-tier university in China. Through statistical analysis of the final exam, students’ common weakness in knowledge acquisition and retrieval is observed and only a small fraction demonstrate transition from novice-like to expert-like problem solving. Thus, the traditional pedagogy of lecturing followed by student-directed learning currently assumed turns out to be insufficient in developing students’ general problem-solving capabilities. Moreover, the practical usage of the Minnesota Assessment of Problem Solving rubric in the assessment indicates that explicit problem-solving instruction is feasible in large-enrolment lectures, which is promising in improving students’ knowledge integration in future teaching practice. © 2025 The Author(s). Published by IOP Publishing Ltd.},
