	abstract = {We present an automated current–voltage (I–V) curve tracer using the open-source Arduino platform, designed explicitly as structured, multi-stage student project for physics laboratory teaching. Retaining the classical circuit arrangement, the system digitizes voltage and current acquisition to generate accurate I–V curves. The DC variable voltage source is realized using the on-board digital-to-analogue converter, with an operational amplifier buffer to boost the output currents up to 250 mA. A digital temperature sensor extends the setup to explore temperature dependence of device characteristics. The curve tracer is validated against a high-end commercial I–V curve tracer and applied to resistors, silicon and germanium diodes, a light emitting diode, a Zener diode, and an incandescent bulb. Each phase of the project is mapped to explicit learning objectives, covering semiconductor device physics, analogue electronics, digital instrumentation, and experimental data analysis. A five-step guided measurement sequence is described, with commentary on the intended learning outcomes at each step, making the system suitable for both introductory and advanced undergraduate laboratory curricula. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the https://publishingsupport.iopscience.iop.org/iop-standard/v1.},
