	abstract = {The popping of inflatable rubber balloons filled with different gases provides a simple, low-cost and pedagogically valuable experiment to investigate the acoustic signatures of gases with contrasting thermodynamic properties. In this study, balloons of identical geometry were inflated with helium, argon, nitrogen, and air, then burst while the resulting acoustic signals were recorded using a smartphone microphone. Power spectral density analysis was performed. The results reveal clear and systematic differences between gases: helium produces high-frequency-rich, broadband spectra with large spectral centroids and bandwidths, whereas argon exhibits low-frequency-dominated spectra with steep high-frequency attenuation. Nitrogen and air show intermediate behaviour, reflecting their similar molar masses and sound speeds. The study demonstrates that acoustic spectral analysis of balloon explosions enables reliable qualitative discrimination between light and heavy gases using simple instrumentation. This approach is robust, non-invasive, and well suited for educational demonstrations and introductory experimental physics activities. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
