	abstract = {In this article, we utilized an experimental and cross-sectional approach to assess the evolution and learning impact of remote labs on the acquisition of meaningful knowledge by engineering students. To accomplish this objective, we automated a device capable of replicating the phenomenon of motion on an inclined plane and incorporated additional features to enable remote control, granting students access to the apparatus. Data for the statistical analysis was collected from a sample of 78 engineering students with similar educational levels and experience, who participated in four stages of the process. Statistical analysis performed through Friedman test strongly points to a positive influence of the remote laboratory in the learning process, in contrast to the use of a traditional one and the conventional theoretical induction. It is important to notice that, in both tests, the significance was less than 0.001, which strongly supports the findings. Satisfaction surveys were applied to the students, whose results showed a high reliability thanks to a Cronbach´s alpha coefficient of 0.9. These results highlight the positive perception of the students about the remote laboratory, compared to other learning models. Moreover, participants were highly satisfied with the integrated pedagogical tool in the class curriculum, valuing its robustness, ease of access, real-time control over main parameters, and user-friendly interface, which facilitated obtaining first-hand experimental data. © 2024 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
