	abstract = {This paper describes a case study of an innovative pedagogical intervention that integrates outdoor experiential learning with foundational physics education within an undergraduate Health and Sports Science (HSS) programme at South East Technological University, Ireland. Supported by the Strategic Alignment of Teaching and Learning Enhancement initiative, the intervention reconceptualises traditional lecture-based instruction by embedding physics in a context-rich, surf-based learning environment designed to address common barriers faced by non-specialist learners, including limited prior exposure to physics, low confidence, and perceived lack of relevance. The intervention draws upon experiential learning theory, active learning pedagogy, and the principles of Education for Sustainable Development, aiming to enhance student engagement, promote inclusivity, and strengthen the interdisciplinary relevance of physics within HSS as part of science, technology, engineering, and mathematics education for non-specialist learners. In this paper, we present the pedagogical rationale, conceptualisation, curriculum design and logistical implementation of the intervention, together with early reflections and qualitative insights gathered during the pilot phase. While a full analysis of student survey data will be reported separately, these initial observations highlight increased student motivation, stronger conceptual connections, and more positive attitudes toward physics. Further work is also underway to present the associated physics learning activities in greater depth. Importantly, the initiative offers a transferable framework for teaching physics to non-specialist learners. The model can be adapted to other outdoor and sporting activities, providing a scalable model for enriching physics education across diverse disciplines and educational levels. © 2025 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologi