	abstract = {Electrostatics is a crucial topic in physics, one that requires learners to understand the meanings of signs used to represent electric charges, forces, and fields. This paper reports findings of a mixed method study that explored 395 physical sciences learners’ understanding and use of positive (+) and negative (−) signs in the topic of electrostatics prescribed in the South African curriculum. A test comprising multiple-choice and calculation-based questions was designed to examine learners’ understanding and use of positive and negative signs across various electrostatic contexts. The study was framed by the categories of the meaning of signs in physics, namely direction, opposition, change, and compound. Quantitative and qualitative data were collected and analysed using descriptive statistics and content analysis. The results revealed that some learners lacked an adequate understanding of signs and were unable to use them to solve various problems of electrostatics. Based on the findings, recommendations for teaching electrostatics were made. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. Original content from this work may be used under the terms of the https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/. Any further distribution of this work must maintain attribution to the author(s) and the title of the work, journal citation and DOI.},
