	affiliations = {Korea Foundation for Science and Creativity, South Korea; Department of Physics Education, Pusan National University, South Korea},
	abstract = {Inquiry-oriented approaches in physics education emphasise students’ engagement in scientific practices. However, school-based inquiry often constrains opportunities for students to organise research content and select methods in relation to their investigative focus. This study examines how research content and research methods are structured and coupled in student-led physics research. We analysed 400 physics Research and Education reports produced in Korea between 2021 and 2025 using a two-track approach that combines topic modelling and dictionary-based method analysis. Topic modelling was used to identify content-topic structures, and method keywords were analysed to examine patterns of method use and content–method coupling. The results show that students’ research topics are organised less around conventional physics subfields and more around the character of phenomena and problems under investigation. Five content-topic categories were identified: motion and dynamics, sound and vibration, force and stability, energy and efficiency, and fields and measurement. Method use was structured more clearly by content–method coupling than by year-to-year trends. Simulation was strongly coupled across all content topics, whereas other methods differentiated topics in systematic ways. For example, FFT and sensor methods were prominent in sound and vibration, CFD and wind tunnel methods in Energy and Efficiency, and laser and sensor methods in Fields and Measurement. These findings suggest that student physics research involves the joint organisation of content and method. The study provides large-scale empirical insight into student research practices and offers implications for supporting physics inquiry learning that connects research questions, content, and methods. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the https://publis