	abstract = {This study presents a low-cost teaching apparatus designed to demonstrate the deflection of charged radiation in electric and magnetic fields under ambient laboratory conditions. The system employs a commercially available thorium-based lantern mantle as a radiation source. Thorium decay series isotopes are known to emit predominantly alpha radiation, as documented in international radiation assessment reports (United Nations Scientific Committee on the Effects of Atomic Radiation (UNSCEAR) 2000 Sources and effects of ionizing radiation (United Nations) www.unscear.org/). A Geiger–Müller detector equipped with a mica window is used for particle detection. Experiments were conducted using electric-field-only, magnetic-field-only, and combined-field configurations. The detector position was scanned laterally to obtain spatial count distributions and observe deflection patterns. The measurements reveal clear shifts in detected radiation corresponding to applied fields, demonstrating the qualitative behaviour of charged particle motion. The observed deflection directions under both electric and magnetic fields are consistent with positively charged particles, suggesting that the dominant deflection behaviour is associated with a positively charged radiation component. Preliminary attenuation tests using paper and thin plastic absorbers suggest that multiple radiation components may be present; however, only the positively charged component produces the systematic peak displacement observed under applied fields. The results highlight the value of the system as an accessible instructional platform for illustrating Lorentz force effects, radiation attenuation, and the differences between idealized textbook models and real experimental conditions. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
