	abstract = {The simple pendulum experiment is a fundamental tool in physics education, illustrating key concepts in mechanics and data analysis. This study presents a comprehensive examination of the experiment, emphasizing the crucial role of metrology – the science of measurement – in obtaining reliable results. We highlight how careful statistical analysis, including assessing the convergence of statistical measures, and uncertainty estimation are essential. A key finding is that even when the calculated acceleration due to gravity (g) is close to the expected value, a detailed analysis of the L vs. T<sup>2</sup> relationship can reveal significant systematic errors, such as those arising from inaccuracies in length or time measurements. By systematically analysing these errors, this paper provides valuable insights for educators and students seeking a deeper understanding of experimental physics and the importance of metrological rigor. © 2025 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
