	affiliations = {Physics Department, US Naval Academy, Annapolis, 21402, MD, United States},
	abstract = {By combining the conventional form of the Bernoulli equation (which assumes isothermal and incompressible flow along a streamline) with the ideal gas law, good agreement between theory and experiment has been recently found for the time dependence of the pressure inside a two-liter plastic bottle initially pumped up to three atmospheres as the air subsequently leaks out of a small hole into the ambient surroundings. However, the predicted flow speed of the air out of the hole can exceed the speed of sound, casting doubt on the incompressible assumption. In this Comment, it is shown that the compressible and isothermal form of the Bernoulli equation gives similarly good agreement, provided that a reduced effective area for the size of the hole is adopted. This compressible model predicts a definite time at which all of the excess air in the bottle will have escaped. On the other hand, for air leaking out of a vessel (such as a spacecraft) into vacuum, one should instead use a compressible and adiabatic model, as has been previously demonstrated. © 2024 IOP Publishing Ltd.},
