	abstract = {The 'super-gun' class of weaponry has been around for a long time. However, its unusual physics has been largely by mainstream physics ignored to this day. We study an example of such a 'super gun', the 'Paris gun'. We first investigate the historic accounts of the firing distance of such a gun and try to reconcile it with our physical understanding of ballistics. This is done by looking into the drag component in the equations of motion for ballistic movement, which is usually neglected, for several air density profiles, later discussing the results. It is found that even the simplest of complications that includes air density, shifts the optimal angle from the schoolbook's 45° angle, ground temperature also plays a major role. While the optimal angle changes by a mere 2° in response to a huge change in ground temperature, the maximal distance is largely affected. Muzzle velocity is perhaps the most influential variable when working within a sensible projectile mass range. This manuscript thus provides a paradigm for future studies and should thus be of interest to a wide readership. © 2019 IOP Publishing Ltd.},
