	abstract = {An experiment was carried out to observe how the magnetic moment of a bar magnet varies with its inclination with the magnetic meridian of the Earth. The experiment employs a bar magnet and a compass needle to map magnetic field lines and locate neutral points due to the combined effect of the magnetic field of the bar magnet and the Earth. Neutral points are obtained where the horizontal component of the Earth's magnetic field is balanced by the magnetic field produced by the magnet. The bar magnet was first placed in the magnetic meridian of Earth with its north pole pointing towards geographical north and then its position was changed making different angles with the magnetic meridian of Earth. As expected, the position of the neutral points changed. The magnetic moment of the magnet was calculated in each case. It was concluded that the magnetic moment of the magnet goes on increasing as the angle between the magnetic axis of the bar magnet and magnetic meridian of Earth increases. Students generally plot the magnetic field lines around a bar magnet kept along any one direction with respect to the magnetic meridian of Earth. Plotting the field lines in various orientations of the bar magnet, they can observe and appreciate how the position of the neutral points changes in the combined field of the bar magnet and Earth. It might facilitate students to 'discover' the Earth's magnetic field themselves. This interesting experiment can be taken up as a project work to enrich students' understanding about magnetism. © 2018 IOP Publishing Ltd.},
