	affiliations = {Extension School, Harvard University, Cambridge, MA, 02138, United States},
	abstract = {This article presents a simple experiment that allows students to determine a slope from a series of measurements rather than from a given equation. They measure heights and widths for a set of paper rectangles, all with the same width. From these data they calculate areas and plot area versus height. This gives a linear plot, the slope of which they are to determine. The majority fail to recognize that the slope has the units of centimetres and but 25% see that the slope is the width of the rectangle. This is, I feel, a consequence of their mathematics courses in which the graphing of equations and calculating slopes are carried out with unitless equations. To reinforce the physical meaning of slope, a second experiment is done which leads to a plot of velocities versus time for a constant acceleration. The slope in this experiment yields the acceleration. Students who have gone through these experiments are more confident in graphing data and understand the physical significance of slope. © 2001 IOP Publishing Ltd.},
