	abstract = {Most A-level students learn something about thermistors. A study of how their resistance varies with temperature leads on to a discussion of charge carriers in semiconductors, and to the use of thermistors as temperature sensors in electronics. However, less attention is paid to the current-voltage characteristic of a thermistor which is heated by its own current. In the limited time available it is inevitable that students will concentrate on more important characteristics, such as those of diodes; however, it is useful to have alternatives for faster pupils, or for those who, knowing some electronics, are already familiar with the devices commonly studied. For such students, the thermistor is well worth investigating. To begin with, its characteristic is a surprising shape, with the novelty of two equilibrium states for the same applied voltage. Experimentally it is interesting because, in trying to plot it, one meets stability problems which do not arise with any other simple device. Also, from a theoretical point of view, the arguments needed to explain the shape of the curve and the instability are quite interesting and illustrate the usefulness of graphs in understanding phenomena.},
