	abstract = {A few years ago the authors wrote a book (Shayer and Adey 1981) in which a model of the way children learn was used to explain why many children had difficulty with some key concepts in science. The book was greeted with the criticism from some quarters that they were labelling children and being deterministic about what they could and could not do. Some teachers found comfort in the book since it provided a theoretical foundation for their everyday experience that, however they may try to make certain ideas relevant, or practical, the ideas remained difficult for many pupils. And some teachers took the message as an opportunity to shrug their shoulders and say 'I always said it was impossible to teach density to 3F, now I have an excuse not even to try'. What the authors will attempt to do in the article, with respect to certain key concepts in physics, is show that (i) to describe an existing situation is not to say it cannot be changed (although describing may be a necessary prerequisite to changing it), and (ii) not only can density be taught to 3F 'in some intellectually honest form' (Bruner 1963), but that in so doing one has the possibility of assisting pupils to develop their general reasoning powers, which could have value far beyond physics.},
