	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/36149030904?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Capillarity is a very old problem in physics: detailed calculations on this phenomenon were carried out as long ago as 1805 by Laplace in his Mecanique Celeste. A naive observer could reasonably suppose that the material currently presented at the elementary level would be well settled and free from error. Well settled it is, but free from error it is not. For at least 100 years elementary texts have presented two different physical explanations for the rise (or depression) of a liquid in a small bore cyclindrical tube held vertically with one end immersed in the liquid. Since both explanations lead to the same equation for the equilibrium situation it is tacitly assumed that both explanations are correct and (somehow) equivalent. The author points out that the two explanations are not equivalent and demonstrates by reason and experiment which one is inadmissible. This simple example gives an opportunity to reflect on what is required of an 'explanation' in physics.},
