	abstract = {For most of the last 300 years, Newton has been regarded as the greatest scientist of all time, and Mathematical Principles of Natural Philosophy as his greatest work. Everyone exposed to the European scientific culture has been told since childhood how great Newton was. It is very difficult to escape this traditional enthusiasm, a challenge to which would be regarded as foolishness or sacrilege. The author discusses how the awesome reputation of the Principia was created and maintained. Although modern Newton scholarship has provides a more adequate context in which the text can be studied, new knowledge of the private Newton makes little difference to understanding the later impact of the work. Newton is said to have manipulated attitudes to his work, within his own intellectual circle and more widely. The author shows that this carefully constructed image was perpetuated in the 18th century by the philosophers of the Enlightenment. Historical scholarship once made much of a specifically newtonian tradition, but it turns out that newtonian influence is too diverse and diluted for such a general label to be useful. The author follows the reputation of Principia through a number of 18th and 19th century forms of scientific practice. He looks at how the 20th century revolution in physics has affected the reputation of Newton's greatest text.},
