	affiliations = {Portsmouth Polytech., United Kingdom},
	abstract = {The School Physics in Engineering Project provides classes and teachers with examples of engineering that can be used as an integral part of GCSE Physics work. The development of the project began in 1979 as a search for engineering examples of physics that could be used in 13-16 year-old's physics courses, and that could be produced in such a way as to be widely available for schools. The result is a series of books which have evolved from modules written for class use. In 1979, two fairly large engineering developments were about to start in the authors locality: the building of a cantilever road bridge across a river; and the building of a water authority major water treatment plant, involving a 280 kW electricity power supply, a distribution system and electrical plant and equipment. These provided opportunities for case studies, from the survey stage, through construction, to completion three and four years later. The programme was arranged as 7/10 teaching, in a large 11-18 years mixed comprehensive school and 3/10 as curriculum development. The latter was to enable frequent visits to be made to the sites during industrial working hours, and give time to visit manufacturers of plant and equipment to develop modules for class use, and to have these used and evaluated.},
