	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/36149047369?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Goroka Teacher's Coll., Papua New Guinea Univ., Papua New Guinea},
	abstract = {It sometimes happens that parts of a wave front may become scattered, as for example in diffraction, and then be re-assembled but with differing phase relationships and amplitudes. In these circumstances 'Cornu's spiral' is a familiar tool for explaining in both quantitative and qualitative ways what has happened and why the result is as it is. Modern school physics syllabuses, such as the Nuffield A-level course, use the idea of successive phasor addition to explain the operation of, for example, diffraction gratings, and though Cornu's spiral is not explicitly mentioned, it would need only a small modification to the treatment to arrive at it. The author attempts to provide a conceptual framework which the student of A-level or first year subsidiary university physics may use to understand in a qualitative way what is happening at resonance, and upon which he may, if he has access to a computer, fairly easily build a quantitative understanding.},
