	abstract = {In teaching practical physics at the elementary undergraduate level it is traditional to include a discussion of random errors. The student is taught to express the result of repeated measurement of a quantity X as X approximately=x<sub>n</sub>+or-s/n<sup>1</sup>2/. More sophisticated versions of this formula exist in which n is replaced by (n-1), but it is unwise to give the impression that exact arithmetic is required to estimate errors. It is very effective to find the quartiles of the distribution of measurements: Q <sub>1</sub> (the lower quartile) is that measurement below which one-quarter of the readings lie; Q<sub>2</sub> (the median) is that measurement below which one-half of the readings lie; Q<sub>3</sub> (the upper quartile) is that measurement below which three-quarters of the readings lie. Then the formula is X approximately=Q<sub>2</sub>+or-(Q<sub>3</sub>-Q<sub>1</sub>)/n<sup>1</sup>2/.},
